  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "We have already proved that $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational. Use this fact to prove that $\\sqrt{2}+3$ is irrational.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sqrt{2}+3$ is irrational.\n\nIf it were rational, subtracting 3 would make $\\sqrt{2}$ rational, a contradiction.",
      "worked": "Suppose $\\sqrt{2}+3$ is rational. Then $\\sqrt{2}+3=\\frac{m}{n}$ for integers $m,n$ with $n\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sqrt{2}&=\\frac{m}{n}-3\\\\\\sqrt{2}&=\\frac{m-3n}{n}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe numerator and denominator are integers and the denominator is nonzero, so $\\sqrt{2}$ would be rational. This contradicts the established irrationality of $\\sqrt{2}$. Therefore $\\sqrt{2}+3$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 54
  },
